proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "deposited_count",
      "description": "Remove this aggregate field. In a goal requesting three blue and two green cubes, totals of one through four concealed which color quotas remained outstanding. The recorded deposits were green, green, blue, blue, blue. This breaks SUFFICIENCY: the hidden bin contents cannot be reconstructed from the current tabletop. Replace the total with separate requested-color counts; do not retain a redundant total.",
      "value_type": "count"
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_blue",
      "description": "Include this field when blue is requested. The aggregate count lost the blue contribution to mixed-color quotas, breaking SUFFICIENCY. Hold the cumulative number of blue cubes released into the bin, initially 0. Increment at the first act cycle whose incoming motion contains a completed blue release over the opening, including a release followed by retreat. Do not increment when the opening command is merely chosen. Preserve through all other motions, including button pressing. Example: 2.",
      "value_type": "count"
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_green",
      "description": "Include this field when green is requested. The aggregate count lost the green contribution to mixed-color quotas, breaking SUFFICIENCY. Hold the cumulative number of green cubes released into the bin, initially 0. Increment at the first act cycle whose incoming motion contains a completed green release over the opening, including a release followed by retreat. Do not increment when the opening command is merely chosen. Preserve through all other motions, including button pressing. Example: 2.",
      "value_type": "count"
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_red",
      "description": "Include this field when red is requested, replacing the former aggregate count for red-only goals. Hold the cumulative number of red cubes released into the bin, initially 0. Increment at the first act cycle whose incoming motion contains a completed red release over the opening, including a release followed by retreat; never on the cycle merely choosing the opening command. Preserve otherwise. Example: 3. This keeps the field schema consistent with per-color quota checking without adding an unnecessary aggregate.",
      "value_type": "count"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "deposited_count",
      "description": "Remove the aggregate field and its update instruction. It cannot distinguish outstanding color quotas after cubes disappear into the bin, breaking SUFFICIENCY. Use only the separate counts for colors requested by the goal."
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_blue",
      "description": "For a goal requesting blue, initialize to 0 and otherwise carry forward. The former total did not identify outstanding blue deposits in a mixed-color goal. Increment once only when this cycle's incoming frames show a blue cube carried over the bin and then released as the fingers open, with the hand now empty. Identify color from the cube before release, not from objects remaining on the table. Count even if the resting cube is hidden. Never increment for the command being selected, an already empty hand, a retreat without a new release, or a motion executing no steps. Example: 2."
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_green",
      "description": "For a goal requesting green, initialize to 0 and otherwise carry forward. The former total did not identify outstanding green deposits in a mixed-color goal. Increment once only when this cycle's incoming frames show a green cube carried over the bin and then released as the fingers open, with the hand now empty. Identify color before release. Count even if the resting cube is hidden. Never increment for the command being selected, an already empty hand, a retreat without a new release, or a motion executing no steps. Example: 2."
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_red",
      "description": "For a goal requesting red, initialize to 0 and otherwise carry forward. This replaces the former total in red-only goals. Increment once only when this cycle's incoming frames show a red cube carried over the bin and then released as the fingers open, with the hand now empty. Identify color before release and count even if the resting cube is hidden. Never increment for the command being selected, an already empty hand, a retreat without a new release, or a motion executing no steps. Example: 3."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "Mixed-color goals exposed a SUFFICIENCY failure in the single-color quota rule: a correct aggregate count still led to collecting green after the green quota was complete or abandoning an aligned blue cube. Parse a separate quota for every requested color. Compute each current completed count from its incoming memory plus only that color's release visibly completed in the current motion, exactly once. Only select colors with outstanding quotas; press the button only when every quota is satisfied and the final retreat is complete. For the demonstrated blue-and-green task, collect green while its quota remains outstanding, then blue. Preserve the existing rule to descend onto the already aligned eligible cube instead of changing targets when other cubes appear. Do not treat three aggregate deposits as completion of a three-blue quota.\n\nThe generic accessible-cube preference also failed to settle which of several eligible cubes to approach. This is an action-selection SUFFICIENCY problem, not evidence that another historical memory field is needed. Retain clear-grasp feasibility and persistence of an already aligned selection. Do not replace the unspecified tie-breaker with a universal nearest-robot or uppermost-image rule: those rules contradict other recorded selections. A deterministic tie-breaker reproducing all supplied selections remains unresolved by this minibatch; it should not be presented as established by these reports.\n\nSeveral transfer, release, retreat, and button approaches selected the correct operation but missed its world position. Strengthen calibrated localization rather than adding position memory. Back-project cube top-face centers at approximately z=0.04 and use their world xy for the z=0.02 grasp. Recover the bin opening's geometric center using its elevated rim and visible opposing edges, checking front and wrist views together; do not intersect the dark opening center with the tabletop or substitute the centroid of its unoccluded fragment. Under partial occlusion, fit the opening geometry from visible rim segments and the other current view. The opening reference height must be established from that geometry rather than an invented numerical height. Back-project the gray button-top center at approximately z=0.02, not z=0, and use that xy for approach and press.\n\nMake the distinction between target geometry and measured TCP explicit. After a closed-hand transfer reaches approximately z=0.20, re-localize the opening and command open at [bin_x, bin_y, 0.20], correcting residual lateral error in that waypoint. Add a 3 mm vertical displacement only when the centered target would otherwise produce no motion. After release, retreat open to [bin_x-0.10, bin_y, 0.20], using the visually localized bin center, never current TCP xy minus 0.10. Retain the mandatory retreat after the final deposit, and retain the existing grasp, lift, separate closed transfer/opening, button-close, press, and contact-retraction rules.\n\nRemove the requirement to normalize cube-edge yaw into approximately [-0.7854,0.7854]. Valid recorded edge-aligned grasps exceed that interval. Choose a downward, edge-aligned feasible grasp orientation for the selected cube, preserve it through descent and lift, and preserve the measured grasp orientation during transport. Re-estimate edge alignment for a newly selected cube rather than retaining the previous cube's yaw.",
  "refused": "The initial mixed-color report described the selected cube as blue and proposed starting with blue. The wrist view at approach and the subsequent grasp, transport, and release show green; the first two deposits were green and the next three blue. The blue-first interpretation is therefore rejected. Reports proposing nearest-robot or uppermost-image selection are not promoted to a universal rule: an initial green selection chose a different cube from the robot-nearest one, and a later red selection did not choose the uppermost visible red cube. Exact per-scene target coordinates cannot supply a general visible tie-breaker, so those coordinate-specific prescriptions are not passed on as policy rules. Claims that centered release and bin-relative retreat were wholly absent are also overstated: both existed in the previous instruction. Their demonstrated confusion warrants the explicit target-versus-measured-TCP clarification, not an additional phase field or stored bin position. No numeric bin-rim height is supplied because the reports do not establish one."
}

